        <span class="art-tag art-tag--green">Guide</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="art-title">Stripchat <span class="art-grad">Amazon Wishlist</span> Gifts: What Changed, and the Privacy Catch</h1>
      <p class="art-lead">Stripchat's Amazon Wishlist links are back after being pulled — but this time there's a step in between that wasn't there before. Before the field goes live on your profile, you have to actively confirm you understand that Amazon can share your delivery address with the fan who buys you something. Skip that box, and the link stays hidden. Here's what changed, how to set it up without exposing more than you mean to, and how a studio should handle wishlists across a roster.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="art-body container">
    <div class="art-layout">
      <aside class="art-toc">
        <p class="art-toc__title">Contents</p>
        <ol class="art-toc__list">
          <li><a href="#sec-1">What Changed: Wishlist Links Return With a Privacy Step</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-2">How to Add Your Wishlist</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-3">What "Amazon May Share Your Address" Actually Means</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-4">Building a Wishlist That Gets Bought From</a></li>
          <li><a href="#sec-5">For Studios</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq">FAQ</a></li>
        </ol>
        <a href="#cta" class="art-toc__cta">Get help →</a>
      </aside>

      <main class="art-main">

      <p class="art-intro">A wishlist link turns a fan's impulse to give something into a real, shipped object instead of a tip you'll never see again. Stripchat's Amazon Wishlist feature has been through this cycle before — pulled, then quietly reinstated — and this return comes with a disclosure step that changes how you should think about turning it on.</p>
      <p class="card-date" style="margin:0 0 1.75rem;">EWOhub Editorial Team · Published Sep 10, 2026</p>

      <section class="section" id="sec-1">
        <h2>What Changed: Wishlist Links Return With a Privacy Step</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>Amazon Wishlist links let a fan buy something off a public list and have it shipped straight to you — no token conversion, no cash tip, just a gift the fan picked out themselves. Stripchat's version of this disappeared for a while and has now come back, but not exactly as it was before. The field is live again on model profiles, and it now sits behind a mandatory privacy notice you have to actively accept before the link does anything.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">The notice exists because of how Amazon Wishlist shipping actually works, not because of anything new Stripchat built: when someone buys from your list and has it shipped, Amazon's own checkout can expose your delivery address to that buyer, not just to Amazon. That's always been true of the underlying Amazon feature — Stripchat is now making sure you see the disclosure before you switch the link on, instead of assuming you already know how gifted shipping works.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="sec-2">
        <h2>How to Add Your Wishlist</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <ul class="checklist">
            <li>Build (or reuse) an Amazon Wishlist and set it to public, since a private list won't work for fans clicking in from your profile</li>
            <li>Go to My Profile on Stripchat and find the Amazon Wishlist field</li>
            <li>Paste your wishlist link into the field</li>
            <li>Read and check the privacy notice box — this is the step that's new, and the link stays hidden on your profile until you do it</li>
            <li>Save changes; the wishlist link now appears where fans can see it</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="sec-3">
        <h2>What "Amazon May Share Your Address" Actually Means</h2>
        <div class="glass-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <p>This isn't Stripchat handing your address to anyone. It's Amazon's own gift-shipping mechanism: when a buyer sends something from your wishlist to your address, the order confirmation and shipping flow can surface that address to the buyer, since that's how Amazon's gifting checkout is built. Stripchat can't change how Amazon's checkout works — it can only make sure you're told about it before you link a wishlist to a public profile.</p>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">The practical fix is the same one gift economy creators outside webcamming have used for years: don't put your real home address on the list. Use a PO box, a mailbox rental service, or a package-forwarding address instead. It costs a small monthly fee, but it means the worst-case outcome of a fan seeing your shipping address is nothing worse than "a rented mailbox," not your actual home.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="sec-4">
        <h2>Building a Wishlist That Gets Bought From</h2>

        <div class="reason-card" style="margin-top:1.25rem;">
          <div class="reason-num">01</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Price it in real tip-menu terms</h3>
            <p>A wishlist that's all $200+ items looks aspirational but rarely gets bought — most fans buying a gift are making an impulse decision at roughly the same price point they'd tip. Mix in a real number of items in the $10–$40 range so a fan can act on the impulse immediately instead of saving up for the one $300 item at the top.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">02</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Keep it current and specific</h3>
            <p>A stale wishlist with items you no longer want, or generic entries with no explanation, signals you're not actually paying attention to it. Items tied to something visible in your streams — a specific ring light, a costume piece, a game you've mentioned — convert better than a generic list, because the fan can picture the gift actually mattering to you.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="reason-card">
          <div class="reason-num">03</div>
          <div class="reason-content">
            <h3>Acknowledge gifts on stream</h3>
            <p>A wishlist gift only reinforces the habit if the fan sees it land. A shoutout when something arrives — the same way a tip gets acknowledged — is what turns a one-time gift-giver into someone who checks your list again next month.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section" id="sec-5">
        <div class="highlight-box">
          <h2>For Studios: A Roster-Wide Wishlist Policy</h2>
          <p style="margin-bottom:0;">Left unmanaged, wishlist adoption across a roster ends up patchy — some models never turn it on, others accept the privacy notice without reading it and use a home address without realizing what they agreed to. A studio should treat the privacy step as a required onboarding item, not an optional feature toggle: walk every model through what the address disclosure actually means, and standardize on a mailbox or forwarding-address service the roster can use instead of individual home addresses. Bundling that service as part of roster onboarding costs less per model than each performer solving it separately, and it closes the one real risk this feature reintroduces.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="faq-section section" id="faq">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Do I have to accept the privacy notice to use Amazon Wishlist on Stripchat?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Yes. Until you check the privacy notice box, the wishlist field stays hidden on your public profile, even if you've already pasted in a link and saved it. Accepting the notice is what actually turns the link visible to fans.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Can I use a PO box or forwarding address instead of my real one?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>Yes — Amazon's checkout doesn't require your home address specifically, only a valid shipping address. A PO box, mailbox rental, or package-forwarding service works the same way and is the standard way to keep the address a buyer sees separate from where you actually live.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <button class="faq-chevron" aria-expanded="false">
            <span>Does Stripchat take a cut of Amazon Wishlist gifts?</span>
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none"><path d="M5 7.5l5 5 5-5" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"/></svg>
          </button>
          <div class="faq-body">
            <p>No — a wishlist gift moves through Amazon's own checkout, not Stripchat's token system, so there's no conversion and no platform cut on the item itself. The trade-off is that you're relying on Amazon's shipping process rather than Stripchat's payment rails, which is exactly why the address-visibility notice matters.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="section">
        <h2>Trending right now</h2>
        <p style="color:var(--fg-muted);margin:-0.5rem 0 1.25rem;">The promotion service our clients ask about most this month.</p>
        <a href="/stripchat-promo" class="glass-card" style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;overflow:hidden;padding:0;text-decoration:none;">
